# KiCad footprints for the blinky example.
#
#   manta export --format kicad --footprint-map blinky.fpmap blinky.mantaNets
#
# The parts in parts.manta name packages, not libraries: '@~footprint = R-0603'
# says what the part is, and nothing about what any one EDA tool calls it. This
# file is where that translation lives, so the same library also exports to
# Altium, OrCAD and Allegro without editing a part.
#
# Every entry must be 'Library:Footprint'. KiCad resolves footprints against its
# library table and a bare name will not place; manta rejects an entry here that
# has no library, which is the whole reason for writing the file.
#
# The names below are from KiCad's standard footprint library, version 9.

R-0603      Resistor_SMD:R_0603_1608Metric
C-0603      Capacitor_SMD:C_0603_1608Metric
C-0805      Capacitor_SMD:C_0805_2012Metric
LED-0603    LED_SMD:LED_0603_1608Metric
SOT-23-5    Package_TO_SOT_SMD:SOT-23-5
SOIC-8      Package_SO:SOIC-8_3.9x4.9mm_P1.27mm

# A footprint's pads have to be the pins the part declares -- the map renames,
# it does not translate a pinout. CONN-USB-C declares four numbered pins
# (VBUS, GND, CC1, CC2), so it maps to a four-pad connector. A real USB-C
# receptacle has pads named A1, A4, B1 and so on, and nothing on it would
# answer to '1'; using one here would place the part and connect none of it.
# Giving the example a genuine USB-C means changing the part to declare that
# pinout, which is a question about the design rather than about export.
USB-C-16    Connector_PinHeader_2.54mm:PinHeader_1x04_P2.54mm_Vertical
